{
  "format": "draftyak.vectors.sign.v1",
  "description": "ES256 statement signatures. \"signingInputHex\" is the case's signing input: the UTF-8 of \"draftyak/v1/<typ>\\n\" followed by \"jcs\", the statement's canonical JSON, which verifies as received bytes exactly as the statement does. \"signature\" is base64url of the 64-byte r||s and \"publicKeySpki\" base64url SPKI DER with the uncompressed point, both canonical: the same bytes with an unused low bit set, or the point compressed, never verify. Each case verifies as \"valid\" says. A case with \"error\" holds a correct signature over its signing input but breaks a rule: a typ is dotted segments of lower-case letters, digits and hyphens, each starting with a letter, ending in .v and the major version without a leading zero; a statement is a JSON object, and every number in it a safe integer. Building its signing input fails with that code, and it never verifies. A case with \"receivedHex\" gives a statement as received, in bytes that aren't canonical JCS, and a correct signature over \"draftyak/v1/<typ>\\n\" followed by exactly those bytes: a verifier checks the bytes first, so it never verifies. \"keys\" are test-only keys made for these vectors and never used anywhere else; \"signer\" made every signature.",
